# Task 8 — Custom Rules

Custom rules allow John to generate password candidates using predictable patterns.

For example:

```text
polopassword
```

A predictable password pattern might be:

```text
Polopassword1!
```

Instead of manually creating every possible variation, John can generate candidates dynamically using rules.

## Rule File

On the AttackBox:

```text
/opt/john/john.conf
```

A package-manager installation may use:

```text
/etc/john/john.conf
```

## Naming a Rule

```text
[List.Rules:THMRules]
```

`THMRules` is the name of the custom rule.

## Important Modifiers

### `c`

Capitalises the first letter.

```text
polopassword
      ↓
Polopassword
```

### `Az`

Appends characters to the end.

```text
password
   ↓
password1
```

### `A0`

Prepends characters to the beginning.

```text
password
   ↓
1password
```

## Character Sets

```text
[0-9]
```

Any digit from `0` to `9`.

```text
[0]
```

Only the character `0`.

```text
[A-Z]
```

Uppercase letters `A-Z`.

```text
[a-z]
```

Lowercase letters `a-z`.

```text
[A-z]
```

Uppercase and lowercase letters.

```text
[!£$%@]
```

One of the listed symbols.

## Example Rule

```text
[List.Rules:PoloPassword]
cAz"[0-9] [!£$%@]"
```

Conceptually:

```text
c
↓
Capitalise

Az
↓
Append characters

[0-9]
↓
Append a number

[!£$%@]
↓
Append a symbol
```

## Calling a Custom Rule

If the rule is named `THMRules`:

```bash
john --rule=THMRules ...
```

